WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.800
The secret behind any great speech...
it all begins with the speaker,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.040
having somebody
who has that world view,

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.560
who knows what they want to say,

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.400
who knows what story
they want to tell.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.040
That's critical, a speechwriter can
come up with something on their own,

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.200
but it really needs to be
a collaborative process

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:20.480
with the person who's speaking.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.720
We approach any speech

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.280
by asking yourself what story
do we want to tell.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.320
We try to come up with a headline
for the speech first,

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.640
whether it's one, two
or three sentences,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.400
because that's what
you have to hang everything on

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:35.800
otherwise it won't hold together.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.400
Tonight, I want to talk about what
kind of future we're going to have

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.680
and what kind of a nation
we are going to be.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.240
One thing all great speeches
have in common

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.120
is that they're bigger
than the place,

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.120
they say something
bigger than the moment.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.160
Something like
Lincoln's Gettysburg Address,

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.440
he said it wasn't just
to consecrate the ground

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.720
where soldiers gave their lives,

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.400
it was to dedicate ourselves

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.120
to making sure that a nation
conceived in liberty could endure.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.720
So any great speech
has to speak to something bigger

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:05.800
than that particular moment,

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.800
you have to look beyond the headline
you're going to get the next day,

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.560
you have to look beyond tweets.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.560
We wouldn’t necessarily write
speeches with an eye towards

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.360
what people will say
about it in 50 years,

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.480
but you're thinking about the broader
sweep of history and where you fit.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.160
Today, in the world of freedom,

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.040
the proudest boast is,

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.760
Ich bin ein Berliner.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.120
You know, other things that make
a great speech are authenticity.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.720
And authenticity is, you know,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.360
it's something
more than just the truth,

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.440
it's actually saying what's true,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.960
and I just don’t mean the facts,
I mean things that people know,

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.800
the way people speak
in their everyday lives.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.280
Not every great speech has to be
up here like Lincoln or Kennedy,

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.120
and people can tell when
you're trying too hard.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:53.280
A lot of times great speeches

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:54.920
are just talking to people
where they are.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.640
Your fervour, your energy,
your courage,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.240
always is something that's going
to carry me forth.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.200
Humour goes a long way in speeches,
but it's also very hard.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.040
President Obama would also say,
"Give me some more jokes in here",

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.040
and comedy's difficult.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.240
We were always very lucky

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.400
that President Obama had
a great sense of comic timing.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.280
And he knew it, but he was always
good at telling jokes.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:23.280
He relished...

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.520
he'd always dig at himself first
in a self-deprecating way,

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.200
just to open it up so that he could
dig on somebody else.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.720
I am told that the last
three speakers here

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.040
have been the Pope,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.720
Her Majesty the Queen,

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.000
and Nelson Mandela,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.840
which is either a very high bar

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.320
or the beginning
of a very funny joke.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.560
You want to know where you are
and who you're talking to,

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.720
even if it's as small
as visiting a high school

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.160
and knowing who the mascot is.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.720
That handshake at the top
of the speech goes a long way,

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.760
and then you have
the permission structure

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.880
to take the audience
on a greater ride.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.080
I want to thank
all of the students here.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.920
The smart, powerful, creative,
accomplished, young women

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.960
of Mulberry School for Girls,
you all are beautiful.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.320
Any aspiring speechwriter should
just start writing, constantly.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.200
Whether it's journalling,

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.240
whether it’s preparing a speech you
wish you had the chance to write

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:29.840
so that you have something
to show somebody.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:31.480
I'd also say read widely.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.560
That's where empathy comes from,

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.320
is understanding other people's lives
and world views beyond your own.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.440
I don’t have any classic training
in speechwriting,

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.240
I started out as a pre-med student

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.680
and chemistry changed that course
pretty quickly.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.360
But I would tell students,
take a lot of history courses.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.880
It doesn’t always have to be
writing and composition.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:53.320
History, economics, philosophy

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.520
because you're going to pull
from all those disciplines

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:58.280
in any good political speech.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.720
And with that I just have two more
words to say...

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.400
Obama out.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.580
Thanks for watching.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:15.520
Don't forget to subscribe and click the bell to receive notifications for new videos.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:16.880
See you again soon!

